-- ── 2. Reasoning (and sources) for moves + the one confirmed carry ───────────
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Buckel co-sponsored the Angel Investor Tax Credit (HB1256, 2026), a capped (33% of investment, up to $250,000) state tax credit aimed specifically at investment in early-stage/startup Maryland companies -- a small-business growth incentive, not a subsidy to attract large outside employers. His other co-sponsorship, the Corporate Income Tax Rate Reduction Act, is a broad-based rate cut applying to all corporations and is off-axis (not a targeted attraction incentive), so it does not support a higher chair. This fits chair 2: helping small/local businesses grow while not championing large-employer subsidy deals.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/hb1256?ys=2026RS$u$,$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/hb0690?ys=2026RS$u$,$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/buckel01$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='5260bd6f-e70a-46f1-aa7d-49eaf22192cf' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Jason C. Buckel
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Rosapepe sponsored the Transformational Project Financing Program (SB0455, 2026), which creates a structured, state-administered financing mechanism through the Dept. of Housing and Community Development for 'major development projects' via designated 'State-supported development districts' (passed the Senate 43-0), and co-sponsored converting the Biotechnology Investment Incentive Tax Credit into a targeted grant program for the tech sector. This is active, government-offered incentive/financing to attract major projects and employers, administered through a defined structured program rather than an open-ended blank check -- consistent with chair 4's 'large tax breaks and infrastructure... but keep limits.' No source documents the unconditional, no-limits posture required for chair 5.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/sb0455?ys=2026RS$u$,$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/rosapepe$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='9c400214-f007-4a8d-92fe-5f5d23b3838e' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Jim Rosapepe
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Doan's business improvement districts (Monterey Corridor, Tully/Eastridge) are funded by property-owner assessments, not city subsidies, and his direct actions are small business grants on Story Road and funding for a business association. He explicitly rejected a subsidy approach, saying he wants to "give people opportunity to grow... I don't want to give people empty checks" -- this fits helping small/local businesses without subsidizing large outside employers, not the large-incentive chair 4.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://biendoan.com/issues$u$,$u$https://sanjosespotlight.com/san-jose-business-corridor-forms-district-to-fund-improvements/$u$,$u$https://sanjosespotlight.com/the-first-six-months-san-jose-councilmember-bien-doan/$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='e4ac6674-1fa3-422a-857f-570873b86da3' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Bien Doan
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Mahmood's Mid-Market revitalization plan explicitly avoids the tax-break model that once attracted Twitter, instead raising private philanthropic funds; he supports small business grants. The transfer tax cut he co-sponsored is a broad across-the-board rate reduction on real estate sales, not a targeted incentive package with job-quality or hiring conditions, so it does not establish a large-employer-incentive stance. Record best fits supporting small/local business without subsidizing large outside companies.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://sfstandard.com/2026/02/19/san-francisco-theater-mid-market-bilal-mahmood/$u$,$u$https://missionlocal.org/2026/03/sf-lurie-mahmood-transfer-tax-cut-housing/$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='d3c5004c-9ca0-444e-96d9-107d4315abcb' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Bilal Mahmood
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Sauter's platform centers on cutting red tape and removing fees for small businesses and directing city legislation and resources to support existing Polk Street merchants and help new small businesses open in vacant storefronts, rather than offering incentives to recruit large outside employers. He chairs SF's Downtown Revitalization and Economic Recovery Financing District but has no documented large-incentive package for a specific major employer, so the new scale's small/local-business-support chair (2) fits better than the large-employer-incentive chair (4) he was seated at.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://dannyd3.squarespace.com/issues$u$,$u$https://sfbos.archive.sf.gov/supervisor-sauter-district-3$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='d1a320a9-39e9-4152-85a0-11cab602fdc9' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Danny Sauter
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Bailey's legislative record centers on public funds supporting existing regional industries -- sponsoring the Chesapeake Bay Enhancement Program/Maryland Seafood Industry Financial Assistance Fund and farm-brewery licensing reform -- aimed at growing local aquaculture and agricultural businesses rather than large incentive packages to recruit outside major employers. This fits the new scale's small/local-business-support chair (2) better than the large-employer-incentive chair (4) he was seated at.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/bailey01$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='0abc8345-1fbb-4994-b39c-c3c4f4eefc9f' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Jack Bailey
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Jacobs' economic-development record is targeted tax credits and financial assistance for existing small/local agriculture and seafood operations (Enhanced Agricultural Management Equipment Subtraction, Angel Investor Tax Credit, Chesapeake Bay seafood industry financial assistance fund), not a named incentive package aimed at recruiting a large outside employer. His broader Corporate Income Tax Rate Reduction co-sponsorship is a general tax-cut position and does not itself establish an incentive-to-attract-employers stance, so the record best fits helping small/local businesses grow without subsidizing large outside companies.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/jacobs%20j$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='8b43dd9c-26c3-48bb-ac60-d95f8a39349a' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Jay A. Jacobs
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Ghrist's signature economic-development bill, the Rural Readiness Program and Rural Maryland Capacity Building Fund (HB0461/CH0543), funds rural community planning capacity and collaborative local initiatives rather than tax breaks or subsidies for a specific large outside employer, and his companion bond initiatives fund food hubs and small-business resource centers in Eastern Shore counties. This is targeted support for small/local business and community capacity, not an incentive package to recruit a major employer, so it fits helping small/local businesses rather than large-employer incentives.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/ghrist01$u$,$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/hb0461?ys=2026RS$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='eca530ff-628d-417d-a3dc-b858dc7c2376' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Jefferson L. Ghrist
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$The specific economic-development action documented for Lee is his office 'providing grants to businesses that struggled during the pandemic,' i.e. direct relief targeted at small local businesses, not a named tax-abatement or subsidy package to recruit a large outside employer to his district. His broader reputation as a business-community partner and Chamber-backed candidate reflects a pro-business orientation generally, but the concrete action on record is small-business support rather than large-employer incentives.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://laist.com/news/politics/outside-spending-to-reelect-john-lee-tops-1m-as-the-city-councilmember-remains-under-ethics-scrutiny$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='c3155cf3-9a97-43d1-a076-dd6ef6aa46e9' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- John Lee
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$She paired support for cutting regulatory startup burdens on small businesses with an explicit stance that redevelopment projects should be funded by private investors rather than the city — i.e., helping local business grow through deregulation while declining to have the city subsidize larger redevelopment/outside-employer deals. That combination fits the 'help small/local business, no subsidies for large outside companies' rung rather than the large-incentive-package rung she was previously seated in.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://racinecountyeye.com/2018/03/07/sandy-weidner-candidate-interview-common-council-district-6/$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='e13f9358-153f-4358-9f31-3238b05f71bb' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Sandy Weidner
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Sherrill's signature 'First Year Free' initiative waives permitting/licensing barriers specifically for new small businesses, and he opposed the CEO/gross-receipts tax on the grounds it harms 'neighborhood-serving businesses like grocery stores and pharmacies.' There is no evidence he supports tax breaks or abatements to attract large outside employers -- his record fits helping small/local businesses without large-employer subsidies.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://missionlocal.org/2026/03/sf-district-2-candidates-stephen-sherrill-lori-brooke-ceo-tax/$u$,$u$https://sfstandard.com/2026/04/07/stephen-sherrill-lori-brooke-district-2-daniel-lurie/$u$,$u$https://www.sf.gov/profile--stephen-sherrill/$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='54e564e7-4788-4913-b75e-95382896d509' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Stephen Sherrill
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Schmidt co-sponsored the Angel Investor Tax Credit (HB1256), a targeted tax incentive supporting investment in Maryland startups/small businesses, along with a narrow sales-tax exemption (HB0909) and a broad corporate tax-rate cut (HB0690) that applies to all businesses rather than a specific large-employer recruitment deal. No evidence shows him offering large, employer-specific incentive packages, so his record best fits support for small-business/startup growth without large-employer subsidies.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/schmidt01?tab=2026RS-legislation$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='55d9d0b6-78a3-460b-97b9-87913ffc8e85' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Stuart Michael Schmidt, Jr.
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Zhang's platform explicitly commits to championing 'local small businesses' and efficient use of city resources, with no documented support for tax breaks, abatements, or subsidy packages to attract large outside employers to Fremont.$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://tricityvoice.com/2024-general-elections-candidates/$u$,$u$https://tricityvoice.com/fremont-moves-forward-with-charter-city-plans/$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='d6d492b6-cbaf-4398-9301-4fbd10da571f' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Yajing Zhang
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Stewart's campaign lists economic development first, pledging to attract and recruit new businesses that provide an excellent tax base and high-paying jobs — active, aggressive recruitment of major employers. No source shows the unconditional, no-spending-limit posture chair 5 requires, so this fits chair 4: offer large incentives to attract major employers, kept within limits.$ctx$, editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='50682ef1-360a-4597-9e1a-eaf43c50673d' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Dan H. Stewart
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Martin campaigns on aggressively attracting business, touting over $500 million in new business (Starbucks, Superior Market, Target centers) brought to her district and a business-friendly perspective. This is active recruitment of major employers, but no source documents an unconditional, no-limits incentive posture, so it fits chair 4 (compete for major employers with large incentives, within limits) rather than chair 5's maximum-unconditional stance.$ctx$, editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='db853cfa-ab6d-4a30-bfbc-fb05b3956970' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Debra Martin
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$As Redevelopment Agency Manager and mayor, Romero negotiated tax-increment Owner Participation Agreements for Cadence, Union Village, and Henderson Hospital and touts incentive-driven private investment as a headline accomplishment — an active incentive user competing for major employers. These are structured, negotiated agreements rather than an unconditional whatever-it-takes posture, so this fits chair 4 (large incentives kept within limits), not chair 5.$ctx$, editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='494202b1-2cf0-4780-b164-7ae84a1c5185' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Michelle Romero
UPDATE inform.politician_context SET reasoning=$ctx$Hornberger co-sponsored HB1595 (2026), which authorizes Maryland counties and Baltimore City to create a special, reduced personal-property tax subclass specifically for qualified data centers -- a named, sector-targeted tax break intended to attract data-center investment, applying from tax year 2026. No job-quality, local-hiring, or clawback conditions are attached to the incentive, and it is a bounded, industry-specific carve-out rather than an unconditional, unlimited giveaway, placing him at chair 4 (large, bounded incentives to attract employers) rather than chair 3 (conditioned) or chair 5 (unconditional, no limits).$ctx$, sources=ARRAY[$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Legislation/Details/hb1595?ys=2026RS$u$,$u$https://mgaleg.maryland.gov/mgawebsite/Members/Details/hornberger01$u$]::text[], editor_id='854fbc06-40fc-458d-b523-20ef8e5ad1b2', updated_at=now()
 WHERE politician_id='96a6d696-50fd-4393-a0f6-19e69dc15716' AND topic_id='eb3d1247-0de1-4b7f-baec-7259861efd53'; -- Kevin B. Hornberger (carry @4, reasoning refreshed)

